<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catuvolcus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/53-bce">53 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Catuvolcus was an elderly king of the Eburones in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> who shared rule with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambiorix">Ambiorix</a>. He joined the rising of 54 BCE that destroyed a Roman force at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atuatuca">Atuatuca</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> returned in 53 BCE to punish the Eburones, Catuvolcus, too old to fight or flee, cursed Ambiorix for the revolt and poisoned himself with yew.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The Eburones were a Belgic people living between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, and were ruled jointly by two kings, Catuvolcus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambiorix">Ambiorix</a>. Little is known of Catuvolcus before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a>. Julius Caesar began his conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> in 58 BCE, and the next year his forces occupied the lands of the Belgae, a confederation of tribes of northern Gaul to which the Eburones belonged.</p><p>His Gaulish name, Catu-uolcos, joins the stem catu-, battle, to uolcos and is rendered as war-wolf. It has an exact parallel in the Middle Welsh cadwalch, hero or warrior, both coming from a Proto-Celtic form, and is related to the name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volcae">Volcae</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On returning from his second invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in late 54 BCE, Caesar sent 15 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-titurius-sabinus">Quintus Titurius Sabinus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-aurunculeius-cotta">Lucius Aurunculeius Cotta</a> to winter at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atuatuca">Atuatuca</a> among the Eburones. According to Caesar, the two kings received messages from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indutiomarus">Indutiomarus</a> of the Treveri urging them to revolt. About a fortnight later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambiorix">Ambiorix</a> attacked the Roman camp and was driven off, then met Cotta and Sabinus, claimed that his people had forced him into war and warned them that a large German force was about to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> marched out, the Belgic forces ambushed them, massacring the troops and killing both commanders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Caesar rebuilt his forces over the winter and in 53 BCE turned first on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambiorix">Ambiorix</a>'s allies among the Menapii, the Nervii and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a>, then devastated the lands of the Eburones; Lucius Minucius Basilus scattered Ambiorix's men with a cavalry attack before they could gather. Caesar claims that Catuvolcus, too old to bear the labours of war or of retreat, then took his own life by means of a yew tree, cursing Ambiorix as he did so for having started the revolt.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cativolcus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They were ruled jointly by two kings, Ambiorix and the elderly <b>Catuvolcus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/eburones">Eburones</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.5,2.9&amp;year=-63" aria-label="Gaul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4618 529 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Catuvolcus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5068 799h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5068 799h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.5,2.9&amp;year=-63">What was here in 63 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
